#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""A swarm: agents working on one goal, talking through a board.

    swarm start [--agents N] [--budget MILLIONS] [--in-project]
                [--row MODEL,SHARE[,IDENTITY[,EFFORT]]]... -- GOAL
    swarm add [--swarm NAME] [--row N]
    swarm stop [--swarm NAME]
    swarm list
    swarm status [--swarm NAME]
    swarm check [--swarm NAME]
    swarm post [--swarm NAME] [--all] TEXT
    swarm role ROLE
    swarm assign TASK OWNER REVIEWER BRIEF
    swarm claim TASK
    swarm submit TASK RESULT
    swarm review TASK supported|conditional|rejected EVIDENCE
    swarm finish achieved|partial|failed SUMMARY
    swarm leave

An agent's shell names who acts (AGENT_BOT, AGENT_BOT_ID, AGENT_TURN) and
the daemon (AGENT_SOCKET); a member's swarm is the one its name is in. With
no AGENT_BOT, the person acts, as the app does for them, and names the swarm.

A swarm is a folder, ~/.agent/swarms/STORE/PROJECT.NAME/: swarm.json says
who its members are, pinned by bot id; board.jsonl is everything said and
done on it, one JSON line each; state.json is what the board adds up to,
kept with the board's length it was folded to, so an act folds only what
was appended since. Every change holds an exclusive flock on board.jsonl
from its first read to its last send, so two claims of one task never both
win, and a stop is never half-crossed by a post. Readers do not lock: they
read whole lines.

Success is one JSON value on stdout; a refusal is {"error", "detail"} on
stderr, exit 1 (2 for usage).
"""
import fcntl
import json
import os
import re
import signal
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time

